n EPA per play and the defense 21st in EPA allowed, so neither side has stood out much yet. What sets them apart is the 2nd lowest pre-snap motion rate and the 3rd highest eight man box rate in the league.","offense":"The Giants offense has been about average by EPA per play so far. They have been one of the most run-heavy teams in the league, passing less often than game situations would predict. Their biggest outliers in how they line up are the 2nd lowest pre-snap motion rate in the league. Jameis Winston has run the passing game and leaned heavily on Malik Nabers, who has drawn 30% of the targets.","defense":"The Giants defense has been a little below average by EPA allowed per play so far. Its strongest area has been takeaways per game, where it ranks 11th, and its weakest has been sack rate, where it ranks 30th. Tremaine Edmunds has led the way with 2 sacks. Schematically, they have the 3rd highest eight man box rate in the league.","special":"The Giants have been about average in how often they go for it on fourth down. Their kickers have made 81% of field goals, which is a little below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into linebackers, about 21% of the money on the books. Their defensive line was among the oldest in the league.","players":"Jameis Winston has played nearly every snap at quarterback and thrown for 957 yards. Malik Nabers has been the clear top target with 30% of the looks and 494 receiving yards. Cam Skattebo has handled most of the work in the backfield with 58% of the carries. On defense, Tremaine Edmunds leads the team with 2 sacks and Jevon Holland has picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"Matt Nagy is calling the offense and Dennard Wilson is calling the defense. Their offense looks most like the 2022 Broncos and the 2022 Saints under Pete Carmichael. Their defense looks most like the 2025 Buccaneers under Todd Bowles and the 2025 Steelers.","trends":"The offense went from 17th in EPA per play in 2025 to 18th so far this season, about where it was. The defense moved from 25th in EPA allowed per play in 2025 to 21st so far this season, a modest improvement. The biggest change in approach was RPO rate, which went from 12% to 3%.","notes":"The 2026 pages are built from data that nflverse updates every week, so they will keep changing until the season ends. Personnel, formations, coverage and pressure numbers are missing for now because that data is only released after the season."}}